1	Introduction to Computer Networks	Introduction to Computer Networks Computer N...	1.1	Introduction to Computer Networks Computer Network tutorial provides basic and advanced concepts of Data Communication & Networks (DCN). A computer network is a set of devices connected through links. A node can be computer, printer, or any other device capable of sending or receiving the data. The links connecting the nodes are known as communication channels. What is a Computer Network? Computer Network is a group of computers connected with each other through wires, optical fibres or optical links Various devices can interact with each other through a network The aim of the computer network is the sharing of resources among various devices There are several types of networks that vary from simple to complex level Distributed Processing Computer Network uses distributed processing in which task is divided among several computers. Instead, a single computer handles an entire task, each separate computer handles a subset. Advantages of Distributed Processing: Security: It provides limited interaction that a user can have with the entire system Faster problem solving: Multiple computers can solve the problem faster than a single machine working alone Security through redundancy: Multiple computers running the same program at the same time can provide security through redundancy Major Components of Computer Network NIC (Network Interface Card) NIC is a device that helps the computer to communicate with another device. Contains hardware addresses for system identification. Wireless NIC: Uses antenna with radio wave technology Wired NIC: Uses cables to transfer data over the medium Hub Central device that splits network connection into multiple devices. Distributes requests to all interconnected computers. Switch Better than Hub as it sends messages directly to specific devices instead of broadcasting to all. Router Connects LAN to internet and connects distinct networks together. Modem Connects computer to internet over telephone line. Stands for Modulator/Demodulator.	["Understand network fundamentals", "Configure and understand routing", "Implement switching concepts"]	[]	1	1	[61]	45	f	t	2025-07-25 07:21:29.902752	2025-08-12 10:58:49.805127	lesson	\N	legacy	f	0	0
2	Network Architecture and Types	Computer Network Architecture Computer Netwo...	1.2	Computer Network Architecture Computer Network Architecture is defined as the physical and logical design of the software, hardware, protocols, and media of the transmission of data. Types of Network Architectures Peer-To-Peer Network All computers linked with equal privilege and responsibilities Useful for small environments, usually up to 10 computers No dedicated server Less costly but security issues exist Client/Server Network End users (clients) access resources from central computer (server) Server performs major operations like security and network management Better security and performance More expensive but scalable Network Types LAN (Local Area Network) Covers small geographical area like building or campus High data transfer rates Private ownership Easy to maintain and install WAN (Wide Area Network) Covers large geographical area across cities or countries Uses public networks like telephone lines Lower data transfer rates compared to LAN Higher setup and maintenance costs MAN (Metropolitan Area Network) Covers larger area than LAN but smaller than WAN Typically covers a city or metropolitan area Can be public or private	["Understand network fundamentals", "Learn about network protocols", "Apply network security principles"]	[]	1	2	[62]	45	t	t	2025-07-25 07:21:29.908105	2025-07-25 07:21:29.909107	lesson	\N	legacy	f	0	0
