4	TCP/IP Model	TCP/IP Model The TCP/IP model was developed ...	1.4	TCP/IP Model The TCP/IP model was developed prior to the OSI model. It consists of four layers that provide standards for internetworking and communication protocols. TCP/IP Model Layers 1. Network Access Layer Combination of Physical and Data Link layers of OSI Defines how data should be sent physically through network Responsible for transmission between devices on same network Protocols: Ethernet, Token Ring, FDDI 2. Internet Layer Also known as Network layer Sends packets from any network to destination Handles logical addressing and routing Main protocol: IP (Internet Protocol) 3. Transport Layer Provides reliable delivery of data Handles error detection and recovery Two main protocols: TCP and UDP 4. Application Layer Combines Session, Presentation, and Application layers of OSI Provides network services to applications Protocols: HTTP, FTP, SMTP, DNS, SNMP Key Application Layer Protocols HTTP: Hypertext Transfer Protocol for web browsing FTP: File Transfer Protocol for file sharing SMTP: Simple Mail Transfer Protocol for email DNS: Domain Name System for name resolution SNMP: Simple Network Management Protocol for network management	["Understand network fundamentals", "Learn about network protocols", "Configure and understand routing"]	[]	1	4	[64]	45	t	t	2025-07-25 07:21:29.91313	2025-07-25 07:21:29.91516	lesson	\N	legacy	f	0	0
5	Ethernet Technology	Ethernet Technology Ethernet is the most wid...	2.1	Ethernet Technology Ethernet is the most widely used LAN technology and is defined under IEEE standards 802.3. The reason behind its wide usability is that Ethernet is easy to understand, implement, and maintain. Ethernet Characteristics Operates in Physical layer and Data link layer of OSI model Uses bus topology generally Protocol data unit is frame Uses CSMA/CD for collision handling Allows flexibility in topologies Advantages of Ethernet Simplicity: Easy to understand and implement Flexibility: Works with wide range of devices and operating systems Reliability: Uses error-correction techniques Cost-effectiveness: Widely available and easy to implement Interoperability: Allows devices from different manufacturers to communicate Security: Built-in encryption and authentication features Manageability: Various tools available for monitoring and control Compatibility: Compatible with wide range of networking technologies Scalability: Can accommodate addition of new devices and users Standardization: All Ethernet devices designed to work together Ethernet Standards 10BASE-T: 10 Mbps over twisted pair 100BASE-TX: 100 Mbps Fast Ethernet 1000BASE-T: 1 Gbps Gigabit Ethernet 10GBASE-T: 10 Gbps over twisted pair	["Understand network fundamentals", "Learn about network protocols", "Apply network security principles"]	[]	2	1	[65]	45	t	t	2025-07-25 07:21:29.91516	2025-07-25 07:21:29.916133	lesson	\N	legacy	f	0	0
18	OSPF Overview	Introduction to Open Shortest Path First protocol	1	OSPF (Open Shortest Path First)OSPF is a link-state routing protocol that uses the Shortest Path First algorithm to determine the best path to a destination.OSPF Features:Link-state protocolUses Dijkstra algorithmSupports VLSM and CIDRFast convergenceHierarchical design with areas	\N	\N	6	1	\N	45	t	\N	2025-08-11 10:31:12.44896	2025-08-11 10:31:12.44896	lesson	\N	legacy	f	0	0
6	Transport Layer and TCP/IP	Transport Layer Protocols The Transport laye...	3.1	Transport Layer Protocols The Transport layer ensures that messages are transmitted in the order in which they are sent and there is no duplication of data. It provides end-to-end delivery of complete message. Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) Connection-oriented protocol Provides reliable data delivery Establishes and maintains connection between hosts Uses acknowledgments to ensure data reception Implements flow control and error recovery Key Features of TCP Segment Numbering System: TCP assigns numbers to each segment for tracking Flow Control: Manages data transmission rate Error Control: Detects and recovers from errors Congestion Control: Prevents network congestion User Datagram Protocol (UDP) Connectionless protocol Faster transmission than TCP No guarantee of delivery Used for time-sensitive applications Lower overhead than TCP Network Layer Services 1. Packetizing Process of encapsulating data from upper layers into network layer packets at source and decapsulating at destination. 2. Routing Process of moving data from one device to another. Network layer specifies strategies to find the best route from source to destination. 3. Forwarding Action taken by router when packet arrives - router forwards packet to appropriate output interface toward destination.	["Understand network fundamentals", "Learn about network protocols", "Configure and understand routing"]	[]	3	1	[66]	45	t	t	2025-07-25 07:21:29.916133	2025-07-25 07:21:29.91769	lesson	\N	legacy	f	0	0
7	Application Layer Protocols	Application Layer Protocols The Application ...	4.1	Application Layer Protocols The Application layer is the closest layer to the end user and provides network services to software applications. It enables users to access network resources and services. Key Application Layer Functions Identifying communication partners: Determines availability of communication partners Determining resource availability: Checks if sufficient network resources are available Synchronizing communication: Manages cooperation between applications Major Application Protocols Domain Name System (DNS) Translates domain names to IP addresses Hierarchical distributed database Essential for internet functionality Uses UDP port 53 File Transfer Protocol (FTP) Used for transferring files between computers Supports authentication Uses TCP ports 20 and 21 Provides reliable file transfer Telnet Enables remote login to network devices Text-based communication Uses TCP port 23 Not secure (sends data in clear text) Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) Used for sending email messages Works with email clients and servers Uses TCP port 25 Text-based protocol Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) Used for network management and monitoring Collects information from network devices Uses UDP ports 161 and 162 Essential for network administration HyperText Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Foundation of World Wide Web Used for transferring web pages Uses TCP port 80 (HTTP) and 443 (HTTPS) Stateless protocol	["Understand network fundamentals", "Learn about network protocols"]	[]	4	1	[67]	45	t	t	2025-07-25 07:21:29.91769	2025-07-25 07:21:29.918654	lesson	\N	legacy	f	0	0
8	Advanced Networking Concepts	Advanced Networking Concepts This module cov...	4.2	Advanced Networking Concepts This module covers advanced networking topics including switching techniques, network protocols, and modern networking technologies. Switching Techniques Circuit Switching Dedicated communication path established Resources reserved for entire communication Used in traditional telephone networks Guaranteed bandwidth but inefficient resource usage Packet Switching Data broken into packets Packets transmitted independently More efficient resource utilization Used in internet and modern networks Message Switching Entire message sent as single unit Store-and-forward mechanism High storage requirements Not suitable for real-time applications Network Performance and QoS Bandwidth: Maximum data transfer rate Latency: Time delay in communication Throughput: Actual data transfer rate achieved Quality of Service: Network performance optimization for specific traffic types Network Security Fundamentals Authentication: Verifying user identity Authorization: Controlling access to resources Encryption: Protecting data confidentiality Firewall: Network traffic filtering VPN: Secure remote connectivity	["Understand network fundamentals", "Learn about network protocols", "Implement switching concepts"]	[]	4	2	[68]	45	t	t	2025-07-25 07:21:29.918654	2025-07-25 07:21:29.919652	lesson	\N	legacy	f	0	0
9	Modern Networking Technologies	Modern Networking Technologies This module e...	4.3	Modern Networking Technologies This module explores advanced networking concepts including routing protocols, network design issues, and emerging technologies. Routing Protocols Overview Types of Routing Protocols Static Routing: Manually configured routes Dynamic Routing: Automatically discovered routes Default Routing: Route of last resort Interior Gateway Protocols (IGP) RIP: Distance vector, hop count metric OSPF: Link state, cost metric EIGRP: Hybrid, composite metric Exterior Gateway Protocols (EGP) BGP: Path vector protocol for internet routing Network Design Issues Reliability: Network fault tolerance and redundancy Scalability: Ability to grow and adapt Security: Protection against threats Performance: Throughput and latency optimization Cost: Implementation and maintenance expenses Management: Network monitoring and control Emerging Technologies Software-Defined Networking (SDN) Centralized network control Programmable network infrastructure Dynamic configuration capabilities Network Function Virtualization (NFV) Virtualized network services Reduced hardware dependency Flexible service deployment Internet of Things (IoT) Connected devices and sensors Machine-to-machine communication New networking challenges and opportunities 5G and Beyond Ultra-low latency communications Massive IoT connectivity Network slicing capabilities Router Types and Applications Home Routers Internet access for residential use WiFi capabilities Basic security features Enterprise Routers High-performance routing Advanced security features Multiple interface types Service Provider Routers Extremely high scalability Carrier-grade reliability Advanced traffic management Virtual Routers Software-based routing Virtualization technology Cost-effective and flexible	["Understand network fundamentals", "Learn about network protocols", "Configure and understand routing"]	[]	4	3	[69]	45	t	t	2025-07-25 07:21:2