div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Baseline Analysis</h3>\\n                    <ul>\\n                        <li>Establishing performance baselines</li>\\n                        <li>Trend analysis and monitoring</li>\\n                        <li>Anomaly detection</li>\\n                        <li>Capacity planning insights</li>\\n                    </ul>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Root Cause Analysis</h3>\\n                    <ol>\\n                        <li>Symptom identification and documentation</li>\\n                        <li>Data collection and correlation</li>\\n                        <li>Hypothesis formation and testing</li>\\n                        <li>Solution implementation and verification</li>\\n                        <li>Documentation and lessons learned</li>\\n                    </ol>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Advanced Tools and Techniques</h3>\\n                    <ul>\\n                        <li>Network simulators and emulators</li>\\n                        <li>Traffic generators and load testing</li>\\n                        <li>Automated monitoring and alerting</li>\\n                        <li>Machine learning for anomaly detection</li>\\n                        <li>Correlation analysis across multiple data sources</li>\\n                    </ul>\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            ", "validation": {"type": "acknowledgment", "message": "Click 'Continue' when you've reviewed the content"}, "points": 10}, {"id": 2, "title": "Interactive Learning", "type": "interactive_exercise", "description": "Apply what you've learned", "content": "\\n            <div class=\\"interactive-panel\\">\\n                <h3>Practice Exercise</h3>\\n                <p>Based on the content you just reviewed, complete this interactive exercise.</p>\\n                <div class=\\"exercise-area\\">\\n                    <p>This simulation uses the troubleshoot.html layout with dynamic content.</p>\\n                    <p>Type: Networking 2</p>\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            ", "validation": {"type": "input_validation", "required_inputs": ["understanding"], "correct_answers": ["yes", "completed", "done"], "message": "Enter 'yes' when you understand the concepts"}, "points": 20}, {"id": 3, "title": "Assessment", "type": "knowledge_check", "description": "Validate your understanding", "content": "\\n            <div class=\\"assessment-panel\\">\\n                <h3>Knowledge Check</h3>\\n                <p>Answer the following to complete this simulation:</p>\\n                <div class=\\"question-area\\">\\n                    <p>Rate your understanding of this topic (1-5):</p>\\n                    <input type=\\"number\\" id=\\"rating\\" min=\\"1\\" max=\\"5\\" class=\\"form-control\\">\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            ", "validation": {"type": "rating_validation", "min_rating": 3, "message": "Rate your understanding (minimum 3/5 to continue)"}, "points": 30}]	{"step_completion_required": true, "minimum_score": 70, "time_limit_minutes": 45}	{"layout": "troubleshoot_style", "interactive": true, "scoring_enabled": true, "progress_tracking": true, "advanced_features": true}	150	25	40	t	["networking", "advanced", "routing", "security"]	\N	\N	\N	\N	{}	combined	{"ui_config": {"show_device_palette": true, "allow_device_placement": true, "show_config_panels": true, "allow_device_configuration": true}}	\N	f	\N
61	Static Routing Configuration	Introduction to Computer Networks Computer Network tutorial provides basic and advanced concepts of Data Communication & Networks (DCN). A computer network is a set of devices connected through links. A node can be computer, printer, or any other device capable of sending or receiving the data. The li...	Network Fundamentals	\N	Beginner	45	Networking 1	\N	\N	["Understand network fundamentals", "Configure and understand routing", "Implement switching concepts"]	\N	\N	t	2	2025-07-25 07:21:29.906093	2025-07-25 07:21:29.906093	[]	\N	[]	[]	0	0	0	0	\N	1	{}	{}	[{"step_number": 1, "title": "Review Lesson Content", "description": "Study the lesson material and understand the key concepts", "instruction": "Read through the lesson content and identify the main learning objectives", "type": "content_review", "content": "\\n            <div class=\\"lesson-content\\">\\n                <h2>Introduction to Computer Networks</h2>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-description\\">\\n                    <p>Computer Network tutorial provides basic and advanced concepts of Data Communication & Networks (DCN). \\n                    A computer network is a set of devices connected through links. A node can be computer, printer, or any \\n                    other device capable of sending or receiving the data. The links connecting the nodes are known as \\n                    communication channels.</p>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>What is a Computer Network?</h3>\\n                    <ul>\\n                        <li>Computer Network is a group of computers connected with each other through wires, optical fibres or optical links</li>\\n                        <li>Various devices can interact with each other through a network</li>\\n                        <li>The aim of the computer network is the sharing of resources among various devices</li>\\n                        <li>There are several types of networks that vary from simple to complex level</li>\\n                    </ul>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Distributed Processing</h3>\\n                    <p>Computer Network uses distributed processing in which task is divided among several computers. \\n                    Instead, a single computer handles an entire task, each separate computer handles a subset.</p>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"benefits-list\\">\\n                        <h4>Advantages of Distributed Processing:</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li><strong>Security:</strong> It provides limited interaction that a user can have with the entire system</li>\\n                            <li><strong>Faster problem solving:</strong> Multiple computers can solve the problem faster than a single machine working alone</li>\\n                            <li><strong>Security through redundancy:</strong> Multiple computers running the same program at the same time can provide security through redundancy</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Major Components of Computer Network</h3>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"component-item\\">\\n                        <h4>NIC (Network Interface Card)</h4>\\n                        <p>NIC is a device that helps the computer to communicate with another device. Contains hardware addresses for system identification.</p>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li><strong>Wireless NIC:</strong> Uses antenna with radio wave technology</li>\\n                            <li><strong>Wired NIC:</strong> Uses cables to transfer data over the medium</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"component-item\\">\\n                        <h4>Hub</h4>\\n                        <p>Central device that splits network connection into multiple devices. Distributes requests to all interconnected computers.</p>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"component-item\\">\\n                        <h4>Switch</h4>\\n                        <p>Better than Hub as it sends messages directly to specific devices instead of broadcasting to all.</p>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"component-item\\">\\n                        <h4>Router</h4>\\n                        <p>Connects LAN to internet and connects distinct networks together.</p>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"component-item\\">\\n                        <h4>Modem</h4>\\n                        <p>Connects computer to internet over telephone line. Stands for Modulator/Demodulator.</p>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            ", "max_score": 20}, {"step_number": 2, "title": "Knowledge Check", "description": "Answer questions about the lesson content", "instruction": "Select the correct answer based on the lesson material", "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Which concept is most important in Introduction to Computer Networks?", "options": ["Computer networks enable device communication and resource sharing", "Networks are only used for internet access", "Networks require expensive hardware to function", "Networks can only connect computers of the same type"], "correct_answer": "A", "max_score": 30}, {"step_number": 3, "title": "Practical Application", "description": "Apply the concepts learned in a practical scenario", "instruction": "Complete the practical exercise based on the lesson", "type": "text_input", "prompt": "Describe a scenario where you would apply network fundamentals in a real-world situation. Include specific examples and explain your reasoning.", "expected_keywords": ["network", "router", "switch", "data", "communication"], "max_score": 50}]	{"0": {"type": "completion", "expected_answer": "completed", "score": 20, "success_message": "Step completed successfully!", "error_message": "Please complete this step.", "hint": "Make sure to read all the content."}, "1": {"type": "exact_match", "expected_answer": "A", "score": 30, "success_message": "Correct! You understand this concept well.", "error_message": "Incorrect. Please review the lesson content again.", "hint": "Look for key terms in the lesson content."}, "2": {"type": "contains", "expected_answer": "network router switch data communication", "score": 50, "success_message": "Excellent! You've demonstrated understanding of the key concepts.", "error_message": "Your answer needs more detail. Include key concepts from the lesson.", "hint": "Include terms related to the main topic of this lesson."}}	{"use_troubleshoot_template": true, "canvas_enabled": true, "live_scoring": true, "lesson_key": "1.1", "source_content": "\\n            <div class=\\"lesson-content\\">\\n                <h2>Introduction to Computer Networks</h2>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-description\\">\\n                    <p>Computer Network tutorial provides basic and advanced concepts of Data Communication & Networks (DCN). \\n                    A computer network is a set of devices connected through links. A node can be computer, printer, or any \\n                    other device capable of sending or receiving the data. The links connecting the nodes are known as \\n                    communication channels.</p>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>What is a Computer Network?</h3>\\n                    <ul>\\n                        <li>Computer Network is a group of computers connected with each other through wires, optical fibres or optical links</li>\\n                        <li>Various devices can interact with each other through a network</li>\\n                        <li>The aim of the computer network is the sharing of resources among various devices</li>\\n                        <li>There are several types of networks that vary from simple to complex level</li>\\n                    </ul>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Distributed Processing</h3>\\n                    <p>Computer Network uses distributed processing in which task is divided among several computers. \\n                    Instead, a single computer handles an entire task, each separate computer handles a subset.</p>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"benefits-list\\">\\n                        <h4>Advantages of Distributed Processing:</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li><strong>Security:</strong> It provides limited interaction that a user can have with the entire system</li>\\n                            <li><strong>Faster problem solving:</strong> Multiple computers can solve the problem faster than a single machine working alone</li>\\n                            <li><strong>Security through redundancy:</strong> Multiple computers running the same program at the same time can provide security through redundancy</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Major Components of Computer Network</h3>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"component-item\\">\\n                        <h4>NIC (Network Interface Card)</h4>\\n                        <p>NIC is a device that helps the computer to communicate with another device. Contains hardware addresses for system identification.</p>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li><strong>Wireless NIC:</strong> Uses antenna with radio wave technology</li>\\n                            <li><strong>Wired NIC:</strong> Uses cables to transfer data over the medium</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"component-item\\">\\n                        <h4>Hub</h4>\\n                        <p>Central device that splits network connection into multiple devices. Distributes requests to all interconnected computers.</p>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"component-item\\">\\n                        <h4>Switch</h4>\\n                        <p>Better than Hub as it sends messages directly to specific devices instead of broadcasting to all.</p>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"component-item\\">\\n                        <h4>Router</h4>\\n                        <p>Connects LAN to internet and connects distinct networks together.</p>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"component-item\\">\\n                        <h4>Modem</h4>\\n                        <p>Connects computer to internet over telephone line. Stands for Modulator/Demodulator.</p>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            "}	100	20	30	t	["networking_1", "network_fundamentals"]	\N	\N	\N	\N	{}	combined	{"ui_config": {"show_device_palette": true, "allow_device_placement": true, "show_config_panels": true, "allow_device_configuration": true}}	\N	f	\N
62	Dynamic Routing Protocols	Computer Network Architecture Computer Network Architecture is defined as the physical and logical design of the software, hardware, protocols, and media of the transmission of data. Types of Network Architectu...	Network Fundamentals	\N	Beginner	45	Networking 1	\N	\N	["Understand network fundamentals", "Learn about network protocols", "Apply network security principles"]	\N	\N	f	2	2025-07-25 07:21:29.909107	2025-07-26 11:35:03.981639	[]	\N	[]	[]	0	0	0	0	\N	1	{}	{}	[{"step_number": 1, "title": "Review Lesson Content", "description": "Study the lesson material and understand the key concepts", "instruction": "Read through the lesson content and identify the main learning objectives", "type": "content_review", "content": "\\n            <div class=\\"lesson-content\\">\\n                <h2>Computer Network Architecture</h2>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-description\\">\\n                    <p>Computer Network Architecture is defined as the physical and logical design of the software, hardware, \\n                    protocols, and media of the transmission of data.</p>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Types of Network Architectures</h3>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"architecture-type\\">\\n                        <h4>Peer-To-Peer Network</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>All computers linked with equal privilege and responsibilities</li>\\n                            <li>Useful for small environments, usually up to 10 computers</li>\\n                            <li>No dedicated server</li>\\n                            <li>Less costly but security issues exist</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"architecture-type\\">\\n                        <h4>Client/Server Network</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>End users (clients) access resources from central computer (server)</li>\\n                            <li>Server performs major operations like security and network management</li>\\n                            <li>Better security and performance</li>\\n                            <li>More expensive but scalable</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Network Types</h3>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"network-types\\">\\n                        <h4>LAN (Local Area Network)</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Covers small geographical area like building or campus</li>\\n                            <li>High data transfer rates</li>\\n                            <li>Private ownership</li>\\n                            <li>Easy to maintain and install</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                        \\n                        <h4>WAN (Wide Area Network)</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Covers large geographical area across cities or countries</li>\\n                            <li>Uses public networks like telephone lines</li>\\n                            <li>Lower data transfer rates compared to LAN</li>\\n                            <li>Higher setup and maintenance costs</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                        \\n                        <h4>MAN (Metropolitan Area Network)</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Covers larger area than LAN but smaller than WAN</li>\\n                            <li>Typically covers a city or metropolitan area</li>\\n                            <li>Can be public or private</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            ", "max_score": 20}, {"step_number": 2, "title": "Knowledge Check", "description": "Answer questions about the lesson content", "instruction": "Select the correct answer based on the lesson material", "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "What should you understand after completing Network Architecture and Types?", "options": ["Computer networks enable device communication and resource sharing", "Networks are only used for internet access", "Networks require expensive hardware to function", "Networks can only connect computers of the same type"], "correct_answer": "A", "max_score": 30}, {"step_number": 3, "title": "Practical Application", "description": "Apply the concepts learned in a practical scenario", "instruction": "Complete the practical exercise based on the lesson", "type": "text_input", "prompt": "Describe a scenario where you would apply network fundamentals in a real-world situation. Include specific examples and explain your reasoning.", "expected_keywords": ["network", "protocol", "data"], "max_score": 50}]	{"0": {"type": "completion", "expected_answer": "completed", "score": 20, "success_message": "Step completed successfully!", "error_message": "Please complete this step.", "hint": "Make sure to read all the content."}, "1": {"type": "exact_match", "expected_answer": "A", "score": 30, "success_message": "Correct! You understand this concept well.", "error_message": "Incorrect. Please review the lesson content again.", "hint": "Look for key terms in the lesson content."}, "2": {"type": "contains", "expected_answer": "network protocol data", "score": 50, "success_message": "Excellent! You've demonstrated understanding of the key concepts.", "error_message": "Your answer needs more detail. Include key concepts from the lesson.", "hint": "Include terms related to the main topic of this lesson."}}	{"use_troubleshoot_template": true, "canvas_enabled": true, "live_scoring": true, "lesson_key": "1.2", "source_content": "\\n            <div class=\\"lesson-content\\">\\n                <h2>Computer Network Architecture</h2>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-description\\">\\n                    <p>Computer Network Architecture is defined as the physical and logical design of the software, hardware, \\n                    protocols, and media of the transmission of data.</p>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Types of Network Architectures</h3>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"architecture-type\\">\\n                        <h4>Peer-To-Peer Network</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>All computers linked with equal privilege and responsibilities</li>\\n                            <li>Useful for small environments, usually up to 10 computers</li>\\n                            <li>No dedicated server</li>\\n                            <li>Less costly but security issues exist</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"architecture-type\\">\\n                        <h4>Client/Server Network</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>End users (clients) access resources from central computer (server)</li>\\n                            <li>Server performs major operations like security and network management</li>\\n                            <li>Better security and performance</li>\\n                            <li>More expensive but scalable</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Network Types</h3>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"network-types\\">\\n                        <h4>LAN (Local Area Network)</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Covers small geographical area like building or campus</li>\\n                            <li>High data transfer rates</li>\\n                            <li>Private ownership</li>\\n                            <li>Easy to maintain and install</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                        \\n                        <h4>WAN (Wide Area Network)</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Covers large geographical area across cities or countries</li>\\n                            <li>Uses public networks like telephone lines</li>\\n                            <li>Lower data transfer rates compared to LAN</li>\\n                            <li>Higher setup and maintenance costs</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                        \\n                        <h4>MAN (Metropolitan Area Network)</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Covers larger area than LAN but smaller than WAN</li>\\n                            <li>Typically covers a city or metropolitan area</li>\\n                            <li>Can be public or private</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            "}	100	20	30	f	["networking_1", "network_fundamentals"]	\N	\N	\N	\N	{}	combined	{"ui_config": {"show_device_palette": true, "allow_device_placement": true, "show_config_panels": true, "allow_device_configuration": true}}	\N	f	\N
63	Router Configuration Basics	OSI Model OSI stands for Open System Interconnection. It is a reference model that describes how information from a software application in one computer moves through a physical medium to the software application in another computer. <div cla...	Network Fundamentals	\N	Beginner	45	Networking 1	\N	\N	["Understand network fundamentals", "Learn about network protocols", "Configure and understand routing"]	\N	\N	f	2	2025-07-25 07:21:29.912127	2025-07-26 11:34:30.867312	[]	\N	[]	[]	0	0	0	0	\N	1	{}	{}	[{"step_number": 1, "title": "Review Lesson Content", "description": "Study the lesson material and understand the key concepts", "instruction": "Read through the lesson content and identify the main learning objectives", "type": "content_review", "content": "\\n            <div class=\\"lesson-content\\">\\n                <h2>OSI Model</h2>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-description\\">\\n                    <p>OSI stands for Open System Interconnection. It is a reference model that describes how information \\n                    from a software application in one computer moves through a physical medium to the software application \\n                    in another computer.</p>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Characteristics of OSI Model</h3>\\n                    <ul>\\n                        <li>OSI consists of seven layers, each performing particular network functions</li>\\n                        <li>Developed by ISO in 1984</li>\\n                        <li>Divides complex task into smaller, manageable tasks</li>\\n                        <li>Each layer is self-contained</li>\\n                        <li>Upper layers deal with application issues, lower layers with data transport</li>\\n                    </ul>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>The Seven Layers of OSI Model</h3>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>1. Physical Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Responsible for actual physical connection between devices</li>\\n                            <li>Transmits raw bit stream over physical medium</li>\\n                            <li>Defines electrical and physical specifications</li>\\n                            <li>Examples: cables, switches, hubs</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>2. Data Link Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Responsible for node-to-node delivery</li>\\n                            <li>Ensures error-free transfer of data frames</li>\\n                            <li>Handles error detection and correction</li>\\n                            <li>Examples: Ethernet, WiFi protocols</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>3. Network Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Responsible for delivery of packets between different networks</li>\\n                            <li>Handles routing and logical addressing</li>\\n                            <li>Determines best path for data transmission</li>\\n                            <li>Examples: IP, ICMP, ARP</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>4. Transport Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Ensures end-to-end delivery of complete message</li>\\n                            <li>Provides error recovery and flow control</li>\\n                            <li>Segments data and reassembles at destination</li>\\n                            <li>Examples: TCP, UDP</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>5. Session Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Establishes, manages, and terminates sessions</li>\\n                            <li>Provides authentication and authorization</li>\\n                            <li>Handles session checkpointing and recovery</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>6. Presentation Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Handles data encryption, compression, and translation</li>\\n                            <li>Ensures data is readable by receiving system</li>\\n                            <li>Manages syntax and semantics of information</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>7. Application Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Closest to end user</li>\\n                            <li>Provides network services to applications</li>\\n                            <li>Examples: HTTP, FTP, SMTP, DNS</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            ", "max_score": 20}, {"step_number": 2, "title": "Knowledge Check", "description": "Answer questions about the lesson content", "instruction": "Select the correct answer based on the lesson material", "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Which concept is most important in OSI Model and Layered Architecture?", "options": ["The OSI model has 7 layers f