other computer.</p>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Characteristics of OSI Model</h3>\\n                    <ul>\\n                        <li>OSI consists of seven layers, each performing particular network functions</li>\\n                        <li>Developed by ISO in 1984</li>\\n                        <li>Divides complex task into smaller, manageable tasks</li>\\n                        <li>Each layer is self-contained</li>\\n                        <li>Upper layers deal with application issues, lower layers with data transport</li>\\n                    </ul>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>The Seven Layers of OSI Model</h3>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>1. Physical Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Responsible for actual physical connection between devices</li>\\n                            <li>Transmits raw bit stream over physical medium</li>\\n                            <li>Defines electrical and physical specifications</li>\\n                            <li>Examples: cables, switches, hubs</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>2. Data Link Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Responsible for node-to-node delivery</li>\\n                            <li>Ensures error-free transfer of data frames</li>\\n                            <li>Handles error detection and correction</li>\\n                            <li>Examples: Ethernet, WiFi protocols</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>3. Network Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Responsible for delivery of packets between different networks</li>\\n                            <li>Handles routing and logical addressing</li>\\n                            <li>Determines best path for data transmission</li>\\n                            <li>Examples: IP, ICMP, ARP</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>4. Transport Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Ensures end-to-end delivery of complete message</li>\\n                            <li>Provides error recovery and flow control</li>\\n                            <li>Segments data and reassembles at destination</li>\\n                            <li>Examples: TCP, UDP</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>5. Session Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Establishes, manages, and terminates sessions</li>\\n                            <li>Provides authentication and authorization</li>\\n                            <li>Handles session checkpointing and recovery</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>6. Presentation Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Handles data encryption, compression, and translation</li>\\n                            <li>Ensures data is readable by receiving system</li>\\n                            <li>Manages syntax and semantics of information</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"osi-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>7. Application Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Closest to end user</li>\\n                            <li>Provides network services to applications</li>\\n                            <li>Examples: HTTP, FTP, SMTP, DNS</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            "}	100	20	30	f	["networking_1", "network_fundamentals"]	\N	\N	\N	\N	{}	combined	{"ui_config": {"show_device_palette": true, "allow_device_placement": true, "show_config_panels": true, "allow_device_configuration": true}}	\N	f	\N
64	OSI Model Overview	TCP/IP Model The TCP/IP model was developed prior to the OSI model. It consists of four layers that provide standards for internetworking and communication protocols. TCP/IP Model Layers ...	Network Fundamentals	\N	Beginner	45	Networking 1	\N	\N	["Understand network fundamentals", "Learn about network protocols", "Configure and understand routing"]	\N	\N	f	2	2025-07-25 07:21:29.914148	2025-07-26 11:33:59.106391	[]	\N	[]	[]	0	0	0	0	\N	1	{}	{}	[{"step_number": 1, "title": "Review Lesson Content", "description": "Study the lesson material and understand the key concepts", "instruction": "Read through the lesson content and identify the main learning objectives", "type": "content_review", "content": "\\n            <div class=\\"lesson-content\\">\\n                <h2>TCP/IP Model</h2>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-description\\">\\n                    <p>The TCP/IP model was developed prior to the OSI model. It consists of four layers that provide \\n                    standards for internetworking and communication protocols.</p>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>TCP/IP Model Layers</h3>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"tcpip-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>1. Network Access Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Combination of Physical and Data Link layers of OSI</li>\\n                            <li>Defines how data should be sent physically through network</li>\\n                            <li>Responsible for transmission between devices on same network</li>\\n                            <li>Protocols: Ethernet, Token Ring, FDDI</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"tcpip-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>2. Internet Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Also known as Network layer</li>\\n                            <li>Sends packets from any network to destination</li>\\n                            <li>Handles logical addressing and routing</li>\\n                            <li>Main protocol: IP (Internet Protocol)</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"tcpip-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>3. Transport Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Provides reliable delivery of data</li>\\n                            <li>Handles error detection and recovery</li>\\n                            <li>Two main protocols: TCP and UDP</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"tcpip-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>4. Application Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Combines Session, Presentation, and Application layers of OSI</li>\\n                            <li>Provides network services to applications</li>\\n                            <li>Protocols: HTTP, FTP, SMTP, DNS, SNMP</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Key Application Layer Protocols</h3>\\n                    <ul>\\n                        <li><strong>HTTP:</strong> Hypertext Transfer Protocol for web browsing</li>\\n                        <li><strong>FTP:</strong> File Transfer Protocol for file sharing</li>\\n                        <li><strong>SMTP:</strong> Simple Mail Transfer Protocol for email</li>\\n                        <li><strong>DNS:</strong> Domain Name System for name resolution</li>\\n                        <li><strong>SNMP:</strong> Simple Network Management Protocol for network management</li>\\n                    </ul>\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            ", "max_score": 20}, {"step_number": 2, "title": "Knowledge Check", "description": "Answer questions about the lesson content", "instruction": "Select the correct answer based on the lesson material", "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Which concept is most important in TCP/IP Model?", "options": ["TCP/IP is the fundamental protocol suite for internet communication", "TCP/IP is only used for web browsing", "TCP/IP requires special hardware to work", "TCP/IP is being replaced by newer protocols"], "correct_answer": "A", "max_score": 30}, {"step_number": 3, "title": "Practical Application", "description": "Apply the concepts learned in a practical scenario", "instruction": "Complete the practical exercise based on the lesson", "type": "text_input", "prompt": "Design a simple network addressing scheme for a small office. Explain your IP addressing choices and subnet design.", "expected_keywords": ["network", "protocol", "data", "communication", "layer"], "max_score": 50}]	{"0": {"type": "completion", "expected_answer": "completed", "score": 20, "success_message": "Step completed successfully!", "error_message": "Please complete this step.", "hint": "Make sure to read all the content."}, "1": {"type": "exact_match", "expected_answer": "A", "score": 30, "success_message": "Correct! You understand this concept well.", "error_message": "Incorrect. Please review the lesson content again.", "hint": "Look for key terms in the lesson content."}, "2": {"type": "contains", "expected_answer": "network protocol data communication layer", "score": 50, "success_message": "Excellent! You've demonstrated understanding of the key concepts.", "error_message": "Your answer needs more detail. Include key concepts from the lesson.", "hint": "Include terms related to the main topic of this lesson."}}	{"use_troubleshoot_template": true, "canvas_enabled": true, "live_scoring": true, "lesson_key": "1.4", "source_content": "\\n            <div class=\\"lesson-content\\">\\n                <h2>TCP/IP Model</h2>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-description\\">\\n                    <p>The TCP/IP model was developed prior to the OSI model. It consists of four layers that provide \\n                    standards for internetworking and communication protocols.</p>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>TCP/IP Model Layers</h3>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"tcpip-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>1. Network Access Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Combination of Physical and Data Link layers of OSI</li>\\n                            <li>Defines how data should be sent physically through network</li>\\n                            <li>Responsible for transmission between devices on same network</li>\\n                            <li>Protocols: Ethernet, Token Ring, FDDI</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"tcpip-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>2. Internet Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Also known as Network layer</li>\\n                            <li>Sends packets from any network to destination</li>\\n                            <li>Handles logical addressing and routing</li>\\n                            <li>Main protocol: IP (Internet Protocol)</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"tcpip-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>3. Transport Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Provides reliable delivery of data</li>\\n                            <li>Handles error detection and recovery</li>\\n                            <li>Two main protocols: TCP and UDP</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                    \\n                    <div class=\\"tcpip-layer\\">\\n                        <h4>4. Application Layer</h4>\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li>Combines Session, Presentation, and Application layers of OSI</li>\\n                            <li>Provides network services to applications</li>\\n                            <li>Protocols: HTTP, FTP, SMTP, DNS, SNMP</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Key Application Layer Protocols</h3>\\n                    <ul>\\n                        <li><strong>HTTP:</strong> Hypertext Transfer Protocol for web browsing</li>\\n                        <li><strong>FTP:</strong> File Transfer Protocol for file sharing</li>\\n                        <li><strong>SMTP:</strong> Simple Mail Transfer Protocol for email</li>\\n                        <li><strong>DNS:</strong> Domain Name System for name resolution</li>\\n                        <li><strong>SNMP:</strong> Simple Network Management Protocol for network management</li>\\n                    </ul>\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            "}	100	20	30	f	["networking_1", "network_fundamentals"]	\N	\N	\N	\N	{}	combined	{"ui_config": {"show_device_palette": true, "allow_device_placement": true, "show_config_panels": true, "allow_device_configuration": true}}	\N	f	\N
65	RIP Protocol Implementation	Ethernet Technology Ethernet is the most widely used LAN technology and is defined under IEEE standards 802.3. The reason behind its wide usability is that Ethernet is easy to understand, implement, and maintain. <...	OSI Model	\N	Intermediate	45	Networking 1	\N	\N	["Understand network fundamentals", "Learn about network protocols", "Apply network security principles"]	\N	\N	f	2	2025-07-25 07:21:29.91516	2025-07-26 11:33:51.099686	[]	\N	[]	[]	0	0	0	0	\N	1	{}	{}	[{"step_number": 1, "title": "Review Lesson Content", "description": "Study the lesson material and understand the key concepts", "instruction": "Read through the lesson content and identify the main learning objectives", "type": "content_review", "content": "\\n            <div class=\\"lesson-content\\">\\n                <h2>Ethernet Technology</h2>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-description\\">\\n                    <p>Ethernet is the most widely used LAN technology and is defined under IEEE standards 802.3. \\n                    The reason behind its wide usability is that Ethernet is easy to understand, implement, and maintain.</p>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Ethernet Characteristics</h3>\\n                    <ul>\\n                        <li>Operates in Physical layer and Data link layer of OSI model</li>\\n                        <li>Uses bus topology generally</li>\\n                        <li>Protocol data unit is frame</li>\\n                        <li>Uses CSMA/CD for collision handling</li>\\n                        <li>Allows flexibility in topologies</li>\\n                    </ul>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Advantages of Ethernet</h3>\\n                    <ul>\\n                        <li><strong>Simplicity:</strong> Easy to understand and implement</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Works with wide range of devices and operating systems</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Reliability:</strong> Uses error-correction techniques</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Cost-effectiveness:</strong> Widely available and easy to implement</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Interoperability:</strong> Allows devices from different manufacturers to communicate</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Security:</strong> Built-in encryption and authentication features</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Manageability:</strong> Various tools available for monitoring and control</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Compatibility:</strong> Compatible with wide range of networking technologies</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Can accommodate addition of new devices and users</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Standardization:</strong> All Ethernet devices designed to work together</li>\\n                    </ul>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Ethernet Standards</h3>\\n                    <div class=\\"ethernet-standards\\">\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li><strong>10BASE-T:</strong> 10 Mbps over twisted pair</li>\\n                            <li><strong>100BASE-TX:</strong> 100 Mbps Fast Ethernet</li>\\n                            <li><strong>1000BASE-T:</strong> 1 Gbps Gigabit Ethernet</li>\\n                            <li><strong>10GBASE-T:</strong> 10 Gbps over twisted pair</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            ", "max_score": 20}, {"step_number": 2, "title": "Knowledge Check", "description": "Answer questions about the lesson content", "instruction": "Select the correct answer based on the lesson material", "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Which statement best describes Ethernet Technology?", "options": ["This concept is fundamental to networking", "This concept is rarely used in practice", "This concept only applies to large networks", "This concept is being phased out"], "correct_answer": "A", "max_score": 30}, {"step_number": 3, "title": "Practical Application", "description": "Apply the concepts learned in a practical scenario", "instruction": "Complete the practical exercise based on the lesson", "type": "text_input", "prompt": "Provide a practical example of how Ethernet Technology concepts would be applied in a real networking scenario.", "expected_keywords": ["network", "protocol", "data", "layer", "frame"], "max_score": 50}]	{"0": {"type": "completion", "expected_answer": "completed", "score": 20, "success_message": "Step completed successfully!", "error_message": "Please complete this step.", "hint": "Make sure to read all the content."}, "1": {"type": "exact_match", "expected_answer": "A", "score": 30, "success_message": "Correct! You understand this concept well.", "error_message": "Incorrect. Please review the lesson content again.", "hint": "Look for key terms in the lesson content."}, "2": {"type": "contains", "expected_answer": "network protocol data layer frame", "score": 50, "success_message": "Excellent! You've demonstrated understanding of the key concepts.", "error_message": "Your answer needs more detail. Include key concepts from the lesson.", "hint": "Include terms related to the main topic of this lesson."}}	{"use_troubleshoot_template": true, "canvas_enabled": true, "live_scoring": true, "lesson_key": "2.1", "source_content": "\\n            <div class=\\"lesson-content\\">\\n                <h2>Ethernet Technology</h2>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-description\\">\\n                    <p>Ethernet is the most widely used LAN technology and is defined under IEEE standards 802.3. \\n                    The reason behind its wide usability is that Ethernet is easy to understand, implement, and maintain.</p>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Ethernet Characteristics</h3>\\n                    <ul>\\n                        <li>Operates in Physical layer and Data link layer of OSI model</li>\\n                        <li>Uses bus topology generally</li>\\n                        <li>Protocol data unit is frame</li>\\n                        <li>Uses CSMA/CD for collision handling</li>\\n                        <li>Allows flexibility in topologies</li>\\n                    </ul>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Advantages of Ethernet</h3>\\n                    <ul>\\n                        <li><strong>Simplicity:</strong> Easy to understand and implement</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Works with wide range of devices and operating systems</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Reliability:</strong> Uses error-correction techniques</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Cost-effectiveness:</strong> Widely available and easy to implement</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Interoperability:</strong> Allows devices from different manufacturers to communicate</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Security:</strong> Built-in encryption and authentication features</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Manageability:</strong> Various tools available for monitoring and control</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Compatibility:</strong> Compatible with wide range of networking technologies</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Can accommodate addition of new devices and users</li>\\n                        <li><strong>Standardization:</strong> All Ethernet devices designed to work together</li>\\n                    </ul>\\n                </div>\\n                \\n                <div class=\\"lesson-section\\">\\n                    <h3>Ethernet Standards</h3>\\n                    <div class=\\"ethernet-standards\\">\\n                        <ul>\\n                            <li><strong>10BASE-T:</strong> 10 Mbps over twisted pair</li>\\n                            <li><strong>100BASE-TX:</strong> 100 Mbps Fast Ethernet</li>\\n                            <li><strong>1000BASE-T:</strong> 1 Gbps Gigabit Ethernet</li>\\n                            <li><strong>10GBASE-T:</strong> 10 Gbps over twisted pair</li>\\n                        </ul>\\n                    </div>\\n                </div>\\n            </div>\\n            "}	100	20	30	f	["networking_1", "osi_model"]	\N	\N	\N	\N	{}	combined	{"ui_config": {"show_device_palette": true, "allow_device_placement": true, "show_config_panels": true, "allow_device_configuration": true}}	\N	f	\N
